---
import ProductLayout from "../components/ProductLayout.astro";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Does this work with Postgres or just SQLite?",
    answer:
      "Both. SQLite is the local dev default for zero-setup; Postgres is what you run in production. The scaffold autodetects and the migrations work on either.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I rebrand it freely?",
    answer:
      "Yes. MIT license. There's a RENAMING.md walkthrough that covers renaming the demo Project entity, swapping the favicon, customizing colors, and a pre-launch checklist.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does it include OAuth / Google sign-in?",
    answer:
      "No, intentionally. Auth strategy is opinionated and project-specific. The bundled bcrypt + session pattern is enough for v1; layer your own provider later.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will my buyers be able to use this?",
    answer:
      "This is a developer tool, not an end-user product. You build your SaaS on top of it. Your customers never see this scaffold, they see whatever you ship.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I get a refund?",
    answer:
      "Yes. 7-day no-questions refund. Email and you'll get it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is multi-tenancy actually enforced?",
    answer:
      "Yes, and the test suite proves it. There are 7 tests confirming for_tenant() scoping, plus HTTP-level tests where one tenant logs in and tries to GET / PATCH / DELETE another tenant's resource by ID, every attempt returns 404 and the target row is unchanged.",
  },
];
---

<ProductLayout
  ogImage="/og/flask-scaffold.png"
  metaTitle="Flask SaaS Scaffold: multi-tenant, billing, idempotent webhooks"
  metaDescription="A production-tested multi-tenant Flask scaffold with enforced query scoping, Stripe billing, idempotent webhooks, super-admin dashboard, and 28 passing tests. $79 one-time, MIT-licensed."
  eyebrow="Download · Flask Scaffold v1.0"
  headline="Build SaaS faster on a multi-tenant Flask foundation that's actually correct."
  subhead="A production-tested scaffold for indie SaaS founders. Signup, billing, multi-tenant data isolation, admin dashboard: wired up and ready to ship."
  pricing="$79 one-time · MIT license · 7-day refund"
  ctaLabel="Buy on Gumroad"
  ctaHref="https://alveyautomation.gumroad.com/l/flask-saas-scaffold"
  problemTitle="Most multi-tenant Flask tutorials get it wrong"
  whatYouGetTitle="What's in the box"
  whoForTitle="Who this is for"
  finalCtaTitle="Skip the boring 3 days. Start shipping."
  finalCtaSubhead="From zip to running locally in under 60 seconds. To deployed on Railway in under an hour."
  faq={faq}
>
  <Fragment slot="problem">
    <p>
      Most multi-tenant Flask tutorials get the data model wrong. They tell
      you to "add a <code>tenant_id</code> column" and call it done, without
      ever showing you the patterns that prevent cross-tenant data leaks at
      scale.
    </p>
    <p>
      This scaffold is the boring, correct version: enforced query scoping
      at the model layer, tested end-to-end, with explicit escape hatches
      for admin-only views. Every line exists because its absence has burned
      somebody.
    </p>
    <p>The five things most tutorials miss:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The <code>for_tenant()</code> query helper</strong> that
        throws if you forget the tenant_id. Most tutorials let you write
        <code>db.query(Model).all()</code> and discover the leak in
        production.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A clearly named escape hatch</strong>
        (<code>all_tenants_unsafe()</code>) for super-admin views, so code
        review can flag every cross-tenant query.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stripe webhook idempotency.</strong> Stripe replays events.
        If your handler isn't idempotent, you'll double-charge customers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>client_reference_id</code> on the Checkout session</strong> to
        map the payment back to the tenant. Without it, you can't reliably
        activate the right subscription.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tests that prove the multi-tenancy guarantee.</strong> Not
        "looks correct", actually log in as one tenant and verify HTTP 404
        when querying another tenant's resource by ID.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="what-you-get">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-tenant data model</strong> with enforced query scoping,         tenant A literally cannot see tenant B's data, verified by tests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email + password signup / login</strong> with bcrypt.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stripe Checkout integration</strong> with idempotent
        webhook handlers (test mode by default, flip to live in 5 minutes).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stripe Customer Portal</strong> for self-serve subscription
        management.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Super-admin dashboard</strong> for support overrides, cancel,
        reinstate, comp customers onto Pro.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>JSON API + server-rendered UI</strong> with Tailwind. No
        build step in dev.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Alembic migrations</strong>, schema versioning works.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Postgres in production, SQLite for local dev</strong>, zero
        setup.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Procfile + railway.toml + Dockerfile</strong>, deploy in
        one push.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pre-commit hooks</strong> with gitleaks, ruff,
        debug-statement detection.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>28 tests</strong> covering signup, login, multi-tenancy
        isolation, Stripe webhook handling, admin role enforcement, and the
        full signup → checkout → active flow.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="who-for">
    <p>This scaffold is for you if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You're an indie founder building your first SaaS who doesn't want to
        spend the first 3 days re-deriving multi-tenancy basics.
      </li>
      <li>
        You're an engineer leaving a big-co job and starting solo who knows
        what good looks like but doesn't want to set it up from scratch.
      </li>
      <li>
        You've been burned by a leaky multi-tenant app and want the
        enforce-at-the-data-layer version next time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>This scaffold is NOT for you if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You need OAuth-only auth, magic-link auth, or SSO out of the box,         the scaffold is bcrypt + sessions; layer your own on top.
      </li>
      <li>You're already on Django (you have your own conventions).</li>
      <li>
        You're expecting a no-code admin like Forest or Retool, this is a
        Flask blueprint, not a CMS.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>
